  script on the page can read is readable by anything else on the page too, including a third-party script or an
  injected one. That's fine if the userId is only ever a label for logging and display. It becomes a real hole the
  moment anything [3mtrusts[0m it to decide what a user is allowed to see or do — at that point a user edits one storage value
  and becomes someone else. Where that line falls changes which approach is even viable, so I'll ask about it, but not
  first.

  First, the question that drives the storage mechanism:

  [1mThe fork:[0m what has to survive what. This is the one choice that's genuinely expensive to reverse, because it sets what
  the rest of the app can assume is there when it starts up.

  - [1mTab session[0m — identity lives until the tab closes; a reload keeps it, a new tab starts logged out. Safest default,
    and the closest match to "logged in right now." Costs you: users log in again more often.
  - [1mAcross browser restarts[0m — identity survives closing the browser; users stay logged in for days. Best experience, but
    it leaves identity sitting on disk on shared machines, and you now need an expiry policy and a real logout that
    clears it.
  - [1mCurrent page only[0m — identity lives in memory, gone on reload. Nothing persisted anywhere, so no security surface at
    all, but it doesn't satisfy "it should persist" — I'm listing it only so the tradeoff is visible.

  My recommendation is tab session. It satisfies "persists" in the sense that matters for forms filled out across a
  visit, it needs no expiry policy, and it's the cheapest to widen later — going from tab-session to across-restarts is
  a one-line storage swap, while going the other direction after users are used to staying logged in is a visible
  regression.
